{"name":"Workout Green","hex":"#bfe6d2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"workout","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An exercise session; a period of physical exercise.","A schedule or program of specific exercises, especially one intended to achieve a particular goal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/workout"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"text":"Exercise or working out is physical activity that enhances or maintains fitness and overall health. It is performed for various reasons, including weight loss or maintenance, to aid growth and improve strength, develop muscles and the cardiovascular system, hone athletic skills, improve health, or simply for enjoyment. Many people choose to exercise outdoors where they can congregate in groups, socialize, and improve well-being as well as mental health."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Cycling_in_Amsterdam_%28893%29.jpg/330px-Cycling_in_Amsterdam_%28893%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exercise"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/BCRL.jpg/500px-BCRL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/BCRL.jpg/1280px-BCRL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/BCRL.jpg/500px-BCRL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Weight training","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABCRL.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Effects_of_exercise_on_the_organ_systems_of_the_body_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Effects_of_exercise_on_the_organ_systems_of_the_body_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Effects_of_exercise_on_the_organ_systems_of_the_body_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Effects_of_exercise_on_the_organ_systems_of_the_body_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Exercise affects many organs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEffects_of_exercise_on_the_organ_systems_of_the_body_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Central_%28cardiovascular%29_and_peripheral_%28skeletal_muscle%29_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg/500px-Central_%28cardiovascular%29_and_peripheral_%28skeletal_muscle%29_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Central_%28cardiovascular%29_and_peripheral_%28skeletal_muscle%29_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg/1280px-Central_%28cardiovascular%29_and_peripheral_%28skeletal_muscle%29_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Central_%28cardiovascular%29_and_peripheral_%28skeletal_muscle%29_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg/500px-Central_%28cardiovascular%29_and_peripheral_%28skeletal_muscle%29_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Central (cardiovascular) and peripheral (skeletal muscle) adaptations to exercise training","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACentral_(cardiovascular)_and_peripheral_(skeletal_muscle)_adaptations_to_exercise_training.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Muscle_protein_synthesis_signaling_cascades.jpg/500px-Muscle_protein_synthesis_signaling_cascades.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Muscle_protein_synthesis_signaling_cascades.jpg/500px-Muscle_protein_synthesis_signaling_cascades.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuscle_protein_synthesis_signaling_cascades.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Resistance_exercise-induced_muscle_protein_synthesis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Resistance_exercise-induced_muscle_protein_synthesis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AResistance_exercise-induced_muscle_protein_synthesis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Aerobic_Anaerobic_Exercise_Adaptations.jpg/500px-Aerobic_Anaerobic_Exercise_Adaptations.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Aerobic_Anaerobic_Exercise_Adaptations.jpg/500px-Aerobic_Anaerobic_Exercise_Adaptations.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Summary of long-term adaptations to regular aerobic and anaerobic exercise. Aerobic exercise can cause several central cardiovascular adaptations, including an increase in stroke volume (SV)[99] and maximal aerobic capacity (VO2 max),[99][100] as well as a decrease in resting heart rate (RHR).[101][102][103] Long-term adaptations to resistance training, the most common form of anaerobic exercise, include muscular hypertrophy,[104][105] an increase in the physiological cross-sectional area (PCSA) of muscle(s), and an increase in neural drive,[106][107] both of which lead to increased muscular strength.[108] Neural adaptations begin more quickly and plateau prior to the hypertrophic response.[109][110]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAerobic_Anaerobic_Exercise_Adaptations.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Woman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg/120px-Woman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Woman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg/250px-Woman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Woman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg/120px-Woman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWoman_running_barefoot_on_beach.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Cruising_on_a_Board.jpg/250px-Cruising_on_a_Board.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Cruising_on_a_Board.jpg/330px-Cruising_on_a_Board.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Cruising_on_a_Board.jpg/250px-Cruising_on_a_Board.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACruising_on_a_Board.jpg"}],"sounds":{"workout":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-workout.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/36/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-workout.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-workout.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/36/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-workout.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-workout.wav.ogg"}],"green":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-green.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/f/f6/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-green.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back